    {
      "id": "node-text-geo",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "text": null,
        "level": null,
        "markdown": "Geometrically, the derivative $f'(a)$ represents the **slope of the tangent line** to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $(a, f(a))$. As $h$ approaches zero, the secant line passing through $(a, f(a))$ and $(a+h, f(a+h))$ progressively rotates and coincides with the unique tangent line at $x = a$.",
        "description": null,
        "subtitle": null,
        "title": null,
        "latex": null,
        "equation": null,
        "formula": null,
        "expression": null,
        "annotation": null,
        "id_tag": null,
        "label": null,
        "highlight": null,
        "step_number": null,
        "explanation": null,
        "left_title": null,
        "left_points": null,
        "right_title": null,
        "right_points": null,
        "callout_type": null,
        "canvas_type": null,
        "purpose": null
      },
      "importance": "supporting",
      "layout_preference": "auto"
    },
    {
      "id": "node-text-phys",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "text": null,
        "level": null,
        "markdown": "Physically, if $f(t)$ represents the position of a particle at time $t$, then the derivative $f'(a)$ represents the **instantaneous velocity** of the particle at $t = a$. More broadly, it quantifies the instantaneous rate of change of a dependent variable with respect to an independent variable.",
        "description": null,
        "subtitle": null,
        "title": null,
        "latex": null,
        "equation": null,
        "formula": null,
