    {
      "id": "node-text-counter",
      "type": "text",
      "content": {
        "text": null,
        "level": null,
        "markdown": "Consider $f(x) = |x|$ at $a = 0$. This function is continuous at $x = 0$ since $\\lim_{x \\to 0} |x| = 0 = f(0)$. However, if we compute the left-hand derivative and right-hand derivative at $0$:\\n- Left-hand derivative: $\\lim_{h \\to 0^-} \\frac{|0+h| - |0|}{h} = -1$\\n- Right-hand derivative: $\\lim_{h \\to 0^+} \\frac{|0+h| - |0|}{h} = +1$\\nSince $-1 \\neq +1$, the limit defining $f'(0)$ does not exist. Thus, $f(x) = |x|$ is continuous at $x = 0$ but not differentiable there due to a sharp corner.",
        "description": null,
        "subtitle": null,
        "title": null,
